From: Nicolas Villar <15879262+nvillar@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 02:40:22 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Duo: publish whether the two panel window policy is
 active

The policy is switched by persist.sys.phh.duo.panels / persist.debug.phh.duo.panels, read once when the default
display is created; changing them only takes effect after a reboot. A client such as Launcher3 that reads the
switches directly disagrees with the window hierarchy after a toggle without reboot.

When it builds the default display's policy on a Duo, DuoDisplayAreaPolicyProvider now sets
sys.duode.panels.active to 1 (two panel hierarchy built) or 0 (platform default). sys.* is system_prop: system_server
may set it (system_server.te set_prop(system_server, system_prop)), every core and app domain may read it
(system_prop is a core_property_type), and it is not persistent, so it always describes the current boot. A failure
to set it is logged and does not affect the window policy.

Not built or run on a device yet; part of the M1 gate prototype.

Co-authored-by: Copilot App <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 .../wm/DuoDisplayAreaPolicyProvider.java      | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+)

diff --git a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/DuoDisplayAreaPolicyProvider.java b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/DuoDisplayAreaPolicyProvider.java
index 292b5c472d01..b718ff6f5cc3 100644
--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/DuoDisplayAreaPolicyProvider.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/DuoDisplayAreaPolicyProvider.java
@@ -65,11 +65,13 @@ public class DuoDisplayAreaPolicyProvider implements DisplayAreaPolicy.Provider
         final DuoPanelGeometry.Spec spec = DuoPanelGeometry.forDevice(Build.DEVICE);
         if (spec == null || !content.isDefaultDisplay
                 || !panelsEnabled()) {
+            if (spec != null && content.isDefaultDisplay) publishActive(false);
             return new DisplayAreaPolicy.DefaultProvider().instantiate(
                     wmService, content, root, imeContainer);
         }
         Slog.i(TAG, "Two panel display area policy for " + Build.DEVICE);
         sActive = true;
+        publishActive(true);
 
         // Root: what the platform default builds, so system UI windows, the IME, home and spanned apps behave as before.
         final TaskDisplayArea defaultTda = new TaskDisplayArea(wmService, "DefaultTaskDisplayArea",
@@ -96,6 +98,23 @@ public class DuoDisplayAreaPolicyProvider implements DisplayAreaPolicy.Provider
                 || SystemProperties.getBoolean("persist.debug.phh.duo.panels", false);
     }
 
+    /**
+     * Read-only runtime state for other components (Launcher3): 1 when the default display was built with the two panel
+     * hierarchy, 0 otherwise. The persist.* switches can change at any time but only take effect at the next boot, so
+     * a client reading them would disagree with the window policy after a toggle without reboot; this property follows
+     * what was actually built. sys.* is system_prop (system_server may set it, every app domain may read it) and is
+     * not persistent, so it is reset at every boot.
+     */
+    static final String PROP_ACTIVE = "sys.duode.panels.active";
+
+    private static void publishActive(boolean active) {
+        try {
+            SystemProperties.set(PROP_ACTIVE, active ? "1" : "0");
+        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
+            Slog.w(TAG, "Cannot set " + PROP_ACTIVE, e);
+        }
+    }
+
     /**
      * Home on a panel: hides every root task of the panel that holds the focused root task, so home (which is
      * transparent where it has no icon) shows through on that panel only and the other panel keeps its app. Hidden
